          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "high",
            "summary": "This is a real conversation-memory toolkit, but it needs Review because sensitive chat archives can be written through an unauthenticated local API and its advertised encryption is not actually strong encryption.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "Reading OpenClaw session files and writing a local memory corpus is proportionate for this skill, but the REST API starts a localhost service with unauthenticated write endpoints, wildcard CORS, no request-size controls, and insufficient path validation for persistent memory files.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "No post-install hook or automatic remote payload execution was found. The optional embedding feature tells users to install sentence-transformers with an unpinned pip command, which expands supply-chain trust when that feature is used.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The skill explicitly triggers on memory and recall requests, which fits the purpose, but it also promotes forever retention of highly sensitive conversation content without clear consent, redaction, or retention defaults.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "Persistent memory storage is expected, and scheduler/backup actions are user-invoked or disabled by default, but the API can persist attacker-supplied records and the weak XOR-based encryption creates a misleading security posture for retained sensitive content.",
                "label": "Persistence & Privilege",
                "name": "persistence_privilege",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The overall artifact is coherent with a long-term memory skill: it archives, captures, searches, exports, summarizes, backs up, and analyzes conversation records. However, it overstates important guarantees by claiming automatic full capture including thought process and AES-256 privacy encryption; the inspected code does not substantiate those claims.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "concern"
              }
            ],
            "guidance": "Install only if you are comfortable keeping local long-term copies of sensitive conversations. Do not rely on its encryption for secrets, avoid starting the REST API unless you isolate it and trust all local callers, review exported reports before opening or sharing them, and configure retention/backups deliberately because archives may persist or be pushed to a remote Git repository.",
            "findings": "[SQP-2] unexpected: The broad archival and export/backup language is partly expected for a memory system, but the README and SKILL.md promote indefinite retention of full conversations without adequate privacy controls or warnings.\n[TP4] unexpected: Most component-level mismatch claims are downgraded because the repository as a whole contains many advertised memory utilities; the remaining artifact-backed mismatch is the unsupported claim of automatic full capture including thought process and the false AES-256 encryption claim.\n[T02] unexpected: Confirmed in scripts/api_server.py: write endpoints require no authentication, responses use wildcard CORS, and the date field is used directly in a Markdown path, enabling persistent unauthorized memory injection if the local service is reachable.\n[T09] unexpected: Confirmed in scripts/privacy.py: the advertised AES-256 feature is implemented as SHA-256-derived repeating-key XOR with a fixed salt and no integrity protection, with passwords accepted on the command line.\n[T09] unexpected: Confirmed in scripts/html_report.py: stored tags and topics from memory files are interpolated into HTML without escaping, so malicious metadata can become active markup in generated reports.\n[T08] expected: The optional sentence-transformers dependency is plausible for embedding search and is not installed automatically, but the unpinned installation guidance should be treated as a supply-chain risk.",
            "agenticRiskFindings": null,
